.TH PING 1
.SH NAME
ping \- send echo requests to a machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ping
[
.B -n
.I count
]
.I host
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ping
sends ICMP echo requests to
.I host
and says which came back.
.I Host
is a name or an address;
.B /net/cs
turns a name into an address out of
.BR /lib/ndb/local ,
so a machine on the bench is pinged by the name the database gives it.
.PP
Each request is one write of an ICMP conversation's
.B data
file under
.BR /net/icmp ,
and each reply is one read of it.
A request nobody answers within a second is reported as lost,
and the next is sent a second after the last.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.BI -n " count"
Send
.I count
requests.
The default is three.
.PP
There is no clock in ring 3 yet,
so no round trip time is reported.
.SH EXAMPLES
Reach the other machine of the bench by name.
.IP
.EX
% ping -n 2 two
64 bytes from two: seq=1
64 bytes from two: seq=2
2 sent, 2 received
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/ping/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR netecho (1),
.IR net (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty when at least one reply came back,
.B lost
when none did, and
.B usage
when the host is missing or the count is not a positive integer.
.SH BUGS
A reply is matched to a conversation by the address it came from,
so two pings of one host from one machine at once may take each
other's replies.
